diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage. End-to-end ICMP between PC4 (172.16.10.4) and Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) is working normally. From PC4 to Laptop0, 5/5 pings succeeded with ~1.43 ms average latency (TTL 63), and the reverse direction from Laptop0 to PC4 also succeeded 4/4 with ~1.35 ms average (TTL 63). Local same-floor connectivity from PC4 to nearby hosts (e.g., 172.16.10.12 and .13) is also healthy with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs. This indicates that the L2 access segments on switch1 and the inter‑VLAN/L3 path (likely via router0) are operating correctly.

Mechanistically, the one-hop TTL reduction confirms routing between 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 is in place, ARP/ND are resolving, and no ACL/firewall is blocking ICMP on this path at present. Localization: the working path traverses PC4’s access port on switch1, through the building uplink, to the inter‑VLAN gateway, and back to Laptop0’s access port on switch1; all components currently pass traffic.

Root cause: No active network fault could be found; the symptom is not reproducible at this time.

Corrective fix: No change required. If the issue recurs, please note the exact time and test both ping and a non-ICMP flow (e.g., SSH/HTTP). We can then check for transient routing/VLAN changes or host firewalls and review router0 inter‑VLAN state and logs around that timestamp. === END ===